      "Fine-tuning continues the training of a pre-trained model on a smaller, task-specific dataset, biasing its responses toward the terminology, formats and reasoning styles in that data. It is more invasive than prompting and more expensive than retrieval, and the decision framework is short: most actuarial tasks do not need it. Fine-tuning becomes attractive in three situations — style adherence at scale, where the task is high-volume and the desired output style is distinctive; vocabulary specialisation, where the domain uses terms the general model misreads, confusing 'loss ratio' with 'loss function' or 'reserve' with the verb; and latency or cost optimisation, where a smaller fine-tuned model matches a larger one on the specific task at lower cost per call.",
