      "Risk and compliance face a structural challenge that intensifies year by year: the volume and velocity of regulatory change has outgrown the capacity of any reasonable team to monitor manually. The risk function has therefore been among the earliest sustained adopters of agentic AI in insurance. Regulatory monitoring is the canonical application — Solvency II, IFRS 17, LDTI under ASC 944, IRDAI circulars and the publications of the International Association of Insurance Supervisors each emit substantial document flows, and an agent that identifies material changes, summarises them in structured form and assesses relevance to existing processes produces a brief that previously required dedicated analyst time.",
      "Capital modelling support has produced measurable returns, assisting with parameter assembly, scenario specification and analytical exhibits while the methodology, assumptions and validation remain under the chief risk officer's and model risk function's control. Stress testing and scenario analysis have evolved alongside the regulatory expectation that they be more frequent, granular and integrated with strategic planning; the agent runs the models, while the choice of which scenarios to model and how to interpret them stays with the risk committee. ORSA production and board risk reporting are document-intensive activities where an agent drafts sections and assembles supporting analyses, compressing the production cycle without touching the substantive view of risk profile and capital adequacy. Model risk management is the application most directly aligned with the actuarial function's professional obligations, supporting a model inventory that has typically grown faster than the function's headcount.",
